(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is facility-associated scabies a particular concern?","choices":["Residents are children under twelve, in whom the infestation spreads through shared headgear","Residents are homeless or displaced, in whom infestation persists through unwashed clothing","Residents travel frequently, carrying the organism between facilities in luggage and bedding","Residents are elderly and immunosuppressed, and a hospital epidemic can follow their admission"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is difficult to eradicate once healthcare workers are infested. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is first-line treatment for vitiligo involving more than 5% of the body surface?","choices":["Topical corticosteroid applied twice daily to all affected areas of skin","Surgical tissue or cellular grafting to repopulate the depigmented skin","Systemic immunosuppression with a calcineurin inhibitor taken orally","Phototherapy, with narrow band ultraviolet B preferred over PUVA"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — PUVA carries adverse effects including increased skin cancer risk. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What should be done with a low-positive herpes simplex type 2 serology result?","choices":["Confirm it with a second method when one is available","Accept it as diagnostic and begin suppressive therapy","Discard it and repeat the identical assay in six months","Confirm it with an immunoglobulin M level on the same sample"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — low-positive results may be false positives. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Which risk factors are named for Rocky Mountain spotted fever?","choices":["Immunosuppression, renal failure, alcoholism and injection drug use","Obesity, diabetes and nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus","Male sex, adults aged 40 to 64, children under 10, and rural dwelling","Female sex, adults over 65 years, urban dwelling, and winter exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is prevalent in southeastern and south central states in spring and early summer. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has suspected scabies with several unexcoriated papules on the wrist. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Potassium hydroxide preparation of a plucked hair examined under a microscope","Skin scraping of an unexcoriated burrow with mineral oil on a number 15 blade","Serological testing for immunoglobulin M and immunoglobulin G against the mite","Punch biopsy of an active lesion sent for direct immunofluorescence studies"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — microscopic identification of the organism, ova or faeces is the definitive diagnosis. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What does the deck say about eradicating human papillomavirus with wart therapy?","choices":["Salicylic acid eradicates it reliably when used for six weeks","Surgical excision eradicates it reliably in immunocompetent patients","No therapy eradicates it with certainty, and recurrence can occur","Cryotherapy eradicates it reliably, so recurrence does not occur"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — set expectations accordingly. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 149"},{"q":"With what other anomalies can naevus spilus be associated?","choices":["Anomalies of vascular, central nervous system or connective tissue origin","Anomalies of the endocrine system with insulin resistance and hyperandrogenism","Anomalies of the gastrointestinal tract with malabsorption and coeliac disease","Anomalies of the haematologic system with monoclonal gammopathy and myeloma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is a variant of congenital naevus present at birth or in the first years of life. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man with folliculitis has failed topical mupirocin. Scraping and culture are being arranged. Which additional test excludes fungal folliculitis?","choices":["A Tzanck smear taken from an unroofed pustule on the thigh","A punch biopsy of normal skin adjacent to an affected follicle","A potassium hydroxide wet mount using a plucked hair","A Wood's lamp examination looking for coral-red fluorescence"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — that is the specific test named for dermatophyte folliculitis. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the most important modifiable association of perioral dermatitis?","choices":["Topical corticosteroid exposure","Sun exposure","Overgrowth of Malassezia yeast","Nickel exposure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and treatment begins by stopping it, with a warning that the eruption may temporarily worsen on withdrawal. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which single feature is characteristic of the molluscum lesion?","choices":["Central umbilication","An advancing scaly border","A thrombosed capillary dot","A shallow painful ulcer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck calls it characteristic. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 134"},{"q":"A patient has a blind-ending track opening onto the skin over the coccyx. Which term describes it, and how does it differ from the alternative?","choices":["A fistula; a sinus instead connects two epithelium-lined surfaces","A sinus; a fistula instead is always congenital in origin","A fistula; a sinus instead is lined with keratin","A sinus; a fistula instead connects two epithelium-lined surfaces"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — both usually arise from a preceding abscess. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which measure is specifically advised against in the management of sunburn?","choices":["Applying aloe vera or a soy-based topical moisturiser","Giving oral or intravenous fluids for systemic symptoms","Popping blisters, because intact blisters are protective","Starting a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug early in the course"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — cool rather than cold water is also specified. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man asks why he has more moles than his sister when they grew up in the same house. Which risk factors are named?","choices":["Older age, cumulative sun damage, tanning and use of hormonal contraception","Ultraviolet radiation exposure, male sex and a genetic component, with more lesions in light skin that sunburns","Ultraviolet radiation exposure, female sex and a purely environmental cause, with more lesions in darker skin","Autoimmune disease, immunosuppression and a family history of thyroid or coeliac disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — numbers peak in the thirties then decline. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A patient's keloid has been treated with laser. Which addition does the lecture say gives the best result?","choices":["Combining the laser with over-the-counter keratolytics","Combining the laser with oral antibiotics","Combining the laser with beta-blockade","Combining the laser with intralesional steroids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — laser works by shrinking collagen or inducing microvascular thrombosis. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which slides did Professor Jaquith name out loud as really important, and what are they?","choices":["The necrotizing fasciitis red flags","The acne treatment ladder, slides 32 and 33","The MRSA risk factor list, slides 44 and 45","The cellulitis versus erysipelas comparison"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — she said 'make sure you know this', which is as direct a flag as the lecture gives. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 15:40"},{"q":"Which trait is shared by the oral agents named across this lecture for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus skin infection?","choices":["Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, clindamycin and doxycycline recur as the core options","Dicloxacillin, cephalexin and amoxicillin-clavulanate recur as the core options","Penicillin V, erythromycin and clarithromycin recur as the core options","Mupirocin, retapamulin and fusidic acid recur as the core options"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with linezolid and ciprofloxacin added in particular settings. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 15-year-old with moderate acne has a family history of keloids. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Wait until the acne resolves on its own before starting any treatment","Begin silicone sheeting over the acne-prone areas now","Avoid all topical acne therapy, since it provokes fibrous overgrowth","Treat the acne early and appropriately"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — acne is one of the named keloid triggers, and early appropriate treatment greatly increases the chance of scar-free healing. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 30-year-old has extensive urushiol contact dermatitis covering both arms, the neck and part of the trunk. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["High-potency topical corticosteroids alone","A five-day course of oral prednisone without any taper","Oral antihistamines alone","High-dose oral corticosteroids tapered over two to three weeks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — extensive disease needs systemic therapy, and early discontinuation causes high rates of rebound. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 61-year-old's melanoma measures 0.9 mm Breslow thickness, but her report notes ulceration and a high mitotic rate. Which is the most appropriate interpretation?","choices":["Sentinel lymph node biopsy is mandatory, because ulceration alone converts this to advanced disease","Sentinel lymph node biopsy should be deferred until the lesion recurs locally after re-excision","Sentinel lymph node biopsy should be offered or discussed, because additional histologic risk factors lower the threshold to 0.8 mm","Sentinel lymph node biopsy is not indicated, because the threshold is a fixed 1.0 mm regardless of other features"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — ulceration, high mitotic rate and lymphovascular invasion are the three named factors. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"When may radiation be used for a keloid?","choices":["Only in patients who cannot tolerate silicone sheeting or compression garments","During the first two weeks after the keloid has been excised","As a first-line treatment before any surgery is attempted","Only once intralesional steroid and compression have both failed over a year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is an adjunct to surgery rather than a primary treatment. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A parent asks whether antibiotics given earlier would have prevented their child's post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Antibiotics prevent it only when the causative organism has been confirmed by culture","Antibiotics worsen it, which is why topical therapy is preferred in young children","Antibiotics do not prevent it, because the immune activation usually precedes treatment","Antibiotics prevent it reliably if started within the first forty-eight hours of the rash"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — that is stated explicitly for this complication. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 46-year-old fair-skinned woman has eighteen months of central facial redness with visible fine vessels across both cheeks and the nose. She flushes with red wine and hot drinks. There are no papules, pustules or comedones. Which subtype is this?","choices":["Phymatous rosacea affecting the nose","Acne vulgaris of adult onset","Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea","Papulopustular rosacea in its early phase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — persistent central erythema, flushing and telangiectasias without papules or pustules, and the most common subtype. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"When should a lichen simplex chronicus plaque be biopsied?","choices":["When emollients alone have failed to settle the plaque completely","When it is atypical, unilateral, nodular, ulcerated or treatment-resistant","When it has been present for longer than six weeks in any location","When the patient reports that the itch is worse at night than by day"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the purpose is to exclude neoplasia or another inflammatory disorder. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 182"},{"q":"What triggers the majority of erythema multiforme cases?","choices":["Sulfonamide antibiotics, accounting for over 50% of cases","Mycoplasma pneumoniae, accounting for over 50% of cases","Idiopathic causes, accounting for over 50% of cases","Herpes simplex virus, accounting for over 50% of cases"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Other infectious causes include Mycoplasma pneumoniae, histoplasmosis, Epstein-Barr virus and coxsackievirus. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is first-line therapy for pityriasis versicolor, and what is one named regimen?","choices":["Oral terbinafine taken for a course of two weeks","Topical nystatin applied twice daily for a fortnight","Topical therapy; selenium sulfide daily for seven days","Oral fluconazole for seven days, in every case regardless of extent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — regimens vary by product. Ketoconazole shampoo or cream, zinc pyrithione, ciclopirox and topical terbinafine are the other options. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Which findings make referral URGENT in squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Cosmetic concern, patient anxiety, a lesion older than one year, a lesion on the trunk, or any pigmented lesion","Only confirmed nodal disease, since high-risk site, size, recurrence and immune status can all be managed routinely","Only failure of two courses of topical therapy, whatever the site, size, histology, immune status or symptoms","High-risk site, size, recurrence, aggressive histology, immunosuppression, neurologic symptoms, or nodal disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — neurologic symptoms suggest perineural invasion. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is finasteride, and what are its cautions?","choices":["An oral antiandrogen used first line in women","A topical 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor applied to the crown, whose effects on libido and erectile function are permanent and do not reverse at all when the drug is stopped","An oral 5-alpha-reductase type 2 inhibitor; about 2% of men report reduced libido and erectile function, reversible on stopping, and it is not indicated in women and contraindicated in pregnancy","A topical vasodilator effective mainly at the crown"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it works best combined with minoxidil. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"Which supportive measure is described as one of the most important considerations in alopecia areata?","choices":["Referral to a wig service before starting any medical therapy","Psychological support, with support groups offered","Referral to endocrinology for thyroid function assessment","Referral for hair transplantation early in the disease course"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the visible nature of the condition carries real psychological weight. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 140"},{"q":"What does the deck say you should do before assigning a diagnosis to a skin lesion?","choices":["Assign the most likely diagnosis first, then look for the features that confirm it — colour, texture, border, size and evolution — and discard those that do not fit","Photograph the lesion and defer all description — type, colour, texture, border, size, distribution and evolution — until the dermatology referral has been accepted","Biopsy every pigmented lesion before any description of type, colour, texture, border or evolution, since morphology is unreliable in premalignant disease","Characterise it systematically — primary lesion type, colour, surface texture, border definition, size, distribution, palpability, ulceration, bleeding, induration and temporal evolution"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — describe before naming. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"One of that 71-year-old's scalp lesions is now thickened, indurated and has bled twice. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure and review in a year, since actinic keratoses commonly involute without treatment","Order a shave or punch biopsy, because induration and bleeding raise concern for squamous cell carcinoma","Treat with liquid nitrogen cryotherapy and review in three months, since these are expected features","Begin field-directed fluorouracil to the whole scalp and review at the end of the course"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — those features are not part of a typical actinic keratosis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which systemic features can accompany cercarial dermatitis?","choices":["Headaches, fever and lymphangitis","Cramping abdominal pain and muscle spasm","Cranial nerve palsy and meningitis","Aplastic anaemia and memory impairment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — pain and swelling peak at forty-eight to seventy-two hours. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Which laboratory and imaging studies are appropriate in classic erysipelas?","choices":["Computed tomography of the affected limb to define the depth of tissue involvement","Punch biopsy of the leading edge to distinguish erysipelas from contact dermatitis","None routinely — the diagnosis is clinical, and blood and tissue cultures have very low yield","Blood and tissue cultures in every case, since organism identification guides the antibiotic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Leukocytosis and raised inflammatory markers are common but not diagnostic. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"An 8-month-old has erythema, scaling and small erosions over the convex surfaces of the buttocks and upper thighs, with the inguinal folds clearly spared. There is no purulence. Which is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Frequent diaper changes, gentle cleansing, air exposure and a thick zinc oxide or petrolatum barrier at each change","A topical antifungal at every change","Oral antibiotics","A high-potency topical corticosteroid applied at every change until the rash has completely cleared"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — reducing moisture and irritant exposure is the foundation, and the fold sparing indicates irritant rather than candidal disease. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 59-year-old has an ivory-white, scar-like plaque on the cheek. He has had no injury there. On careful inspection the pink edge seems to extend further than the visible lesion. Which subtype is this, and what is the concern?","choices":["Superficial basal cell carcinoma, which carries a higher risk of subclinical spread beyond the visible segment","Nodular basal cell carcinoma, which carries a higher risk of subclinical spread beyond the visible segment","Pigmented basal cell carcinoma, which carries a higher risk of subclinical spread beyond the visible segment","Morpheaform or sclerosing basal cell carcinoma, which carries a higher risk of subclinical spread beyond the visible segment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the invisible extension is what makes it dangerous. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How does the imidazole class work, and which two examples does the deck give?","choices":["It blocks synthesis of chitin in the fungal cell wall; nystatin and amphotericin","It inhibits fungal deoxyribonucleic acid synthesis; griseofulvin and fluconazole","It blocks ergosterol synthesis; clotrimazole and ketoconazole","It destroys the fungal cell membrane directly; terbinafine and naftifine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — ergosterol is a vital component of the fungal cell membrane, so blocking it halts growth and replication. The names end in -azole. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A patient asks why you will not prescribe the combination steroid and antifungal cream that a friend recommended. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Combination products cannot be used on the trunk because of the risk of systemic absorption","Corticosteroids prevent the antifungal from reaching the stratum corneum where the fungus lives","Corticosteroids can mask and worsen a dermatophyte infection, producing tinea incognito","Combination products are less effective because the two drugs chemically inactivate each other"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — that is exactly the mechanism of tinea incognito. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Where do plantar warts occur, and what do they form when they cluster?","choices":["On the dorsal foot, and clustered together they form a giant condyloma","On weight-bearing surfaces, and clustered together they form a single deep abscess","On the fingers and palms, and clustered together they form a periungual band","On weight-bearing surfaces, and clustered together they form a mosaic wart"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — mosaic warts are clustered plantar warts. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 145"},{"q":"Which features separate a fixed drug eruption from erythema multiforme?","choices":["It produces extensive epidermal detachment with mucosal involvement and systemic toxicity","It produces tender nodules on the anterior shins that never go on to ulcerate","It recurs at the same site, leaves residual hyperpigmentation, and has few lesions without three concentric zones","It produces wheals that migrate, blanch fully and resolve within twenty-four hours of first appearing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the three-zone target is what defines an erythema multiforme lesion. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Beyond treating before diagnosis, which two actions does the lecture associate with the naturalistic approach?","choices":["Ruling out the worst-case scenario and following responses to interventions","Calculating pretest probability and applying sensitivity and specificity","Assessing readiness to change and arranging follow-up","Formulating a clinical question and appraising the literature"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both are listed under the naturalistic, event-driven approach. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Where does nail unit melanoma most often arise, which sites are high-yield, and is it ultraviolet-driven?","choices":["Most often in the matrix; the thumb and great toe are high-yield sites; it is NOT clearly ultraviolet-driven and may occur in any skin tone","Most often in the nail bed; the index finger and little toe are high-yield sites; it is clearly ultraviolet-driven and occurs mainly in fair skin","Most often in the proximal nail fold; all digits equally; it is clearly ultraviolet-driven and occurs mainly in fair skin","Most often in the hyponychium; the thumb and great toe are high-yield sites; it is driven by high-risk human papillomavirus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — delayed recognition contributes to advanced-stage presentation. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with several solar lentigines wants them removed. Which options are appropriate?","choices":["Narrow band ultraviolet B phototherapy three times weekly","Retinoids, cryotherapy or quality-switched laser","Topical fluorouracil, imiquimod or tirbanibulin","Excision with clear margins under local anaesthetic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — treatment is not necessary, and removal is for cosmetic reasons. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which forms of lentigines are named?","choices":["Lentigo simplex, acral lentigo, agminated lentigo and generalised lentigo","Superficial, nodular, acral lentiginous and lentigo maligna forms","Common, cellular, combined and atypical cellular forms of the lesion","Erythematotelangiectatic, papulopustular, phymatous and ocular forms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — they follow a bimodal age distribution. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has several uniformly tan, well-demarcated macules 5 to 12 mm across on the dorsal hands and forearms, with no scale or induration. Dermoscopy shows finger-like projections and a moth-eaten border. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Lentigo maligna","Seborrheic keratosis","Pigmented actinic keratosis","Solar lentigo"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — uniform pigment on chronically exposed skin with those dermoscopic features. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"How soon do most patients feel relief after scabies treatment, and how long may symptoms linger?","choices":["No relief until a second course has been given at four weeks","Relief in about three days, though rash and itch may last up to four weeks","Relief within hours, with all symptoms gone within three days","Relief only after six weeks, with itch persisting for several months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — triamcinolone can be used for the residual itch. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which specimens are taken in serious cellulitis?","choices":["Blood cultures and a skin punch biopsy","A superficial swab of the intact affected skin","Perilesional tissue for direct immunofluorescence","A potassium hydroxide preparation of the leading edge"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside a complete blood count and creatine phosphokinase. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which reaction to bedbug bites occurs particularly in sensitised patients?","choices":["A bullous reaction","A necrotic ulcer with an eschar","A serpentine advancing trail","Cramping abdominal pain and spasm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the usual reaction is wheals and papules with a haemorrhagic punctum. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is xanthelasma made of, and where does it sit?","choices":["A crowding of sebocytes within an enlarged gland, most commonly on the face","A collection of lipid-laden macrophages forming soft yellow cholesterol plaques, most commonly on the medial eyelids","A collection of keratin within an enclosed epithelial sac, most commonly on the face, scalp and trunk","A collection of mucin extruded from a joint space, most commonly over a distal finger joint"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and it is asymptomatic. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman has several beige to dark brown papules 5 to 15 mm on the trunk that feel warty and look pasted onto the skin. She is worried they are cancers. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["They are benign seborrheic keratoses, diagnosed clinically, and management is supportive","They are premalignant lesions requiring biopsy of each one before any reassurance","They are dysplastic naevi requiring excision because of the risk of melanoma","They are solar lentigines requiring cryotherapy for cosmetic improvement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — they are common in older adults and easily mistaken for neoplasms. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman presents with a rash that shows discrete circumscribed papules and, in the older areas, crusting and scaling. Which conclusion follows from the crusting?","choices":["The epidermis has been affected","The infection is bacterial in origin","The lesions are resolving spontaneously","The subcutaneous tissue is involved"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — crust and scale indicate epidermal involvement, which narrows the reaction pattern. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is the first priority in acquired immunodeficiency syndrome-associated Kaposi sarcoma?","choices":["Begin intralesional vinblastine and radiation — local palliation is the cornerstone of treatment for epidemic disease","Reduce immunosuppressive medication doses — dose reduction is the cornerstone of treatment for epidemic disease","Begin or optimise antiretroviral therapy — immune restoration is the cornerstone of treatment for epidemic disease","Begin liposomal doxorubicin and paclitaxel — systemic chemotherapy is the cornerstone of treatment for epidemic disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — antiretroviral therapy comes before local measures. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which oral antibiotic is described as the drug of choice for impetigo in children?","choices":["Cephalexin","Doxycycline","Dicloxacillin","Erythromycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin is used where there is penicillin allergy. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with several lentigines on the hands asks whether they need treating. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Sun protection alone will make the lesions fade over the course of the following winter","No treatment is necessary; cosmetic removal by cryotherapy or quality-switched laser is an option if she prefers","Excision with clear margins is needed on every single lesion, because of the risk of transformation","Topical depigmenting agents are the only option, since cryotherapy cannot be used at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the diagnosis is clinical and the lesions are benign. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"The lecture contrasts an intuitive system of thinking with what alternative?","choices":["A system that applies only to unstable patients presenting to emergency medicine","A system that avoids all use of probability","A system driven entirely by patient preference","A more deliberate and controlled thought process using logic and probabilities"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs quick, automatic thinking against a deliberate, controlled process that uses logic and probabilities. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man wishes to treat male-pattern hair loss and asks about finasteride. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["It can safely be shared with a female partner who is also experiencing hair thinning at the crown","About 2% of men report reduced libido and erectile function, and these effects reverse when the drug is stopped","The sexual side effects are permanent once they occur","It is most effective at the frontal hairline"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It works best combined with minoxidil, and it is not indicated in women and contraindicated in pregnancy. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"What is the mortality of toxic epidermal necrolysis, and what predicts it at presentation?","choices":["Around 50%, predicted by the presence of the Nikolsky sign","Up to 30 to 35%, predicted by the SCORTEN score calculated within 24 hours","Between 1 and 5%, predicted by the extent of mucosal involvement","Under 1%, predicted by the number of drugs the patient was taking"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and SCORTEN is repeated on day three. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"What is intralesional methotrexate used for in keratoacanthoma, and how does it work?","choices":["Given topically over several months; it slows the turnover of sebocytes within the enlarged sebaceous gland","Given before excision to reduce the size of the lesion; it inhibits deoxyribonucleic acid synthesis in actively dividing cells","Given after excision in order to prevent recurrence; it inhibits fibroblast proliferation within the healing wound bed","Given instead of excision in frail patients; it induces microvascular thrombosis within the vessels feeding the lesion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a chemotherapeutic agent used as a surgical adjunct. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 2-year-old boy has a flat, blanchable, pink-purple patch on one side of his face that stops sharply at the midline. His parents say it has been there since birth, has grown in proportion with him, and is darker when he cries. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cherry angioma","Nevus flammeus","Infantile hemangioma","Nevus simplex"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — present at birth, unilateral with sharp midline cutoff, no involution. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man with Stevens-Johnson syndrome reports gritty, painful eyes on the day of admission. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure and reassess the eyes once the skin has re-epithelialised","Begin topical antibiotic eye drops and review in seventy-two hours","Patch both eyes closed until the acute skin disease has settled","Urgent ophthalmology consultation with slit-lamp examination"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — ocular involvement drives the blinding sequelae of symblepharon and corneal scarring. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Confirmatory (supplemental) testing for human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) is highly specific in order to accomplish what?","choices":["Detect as many infected individuals as possible","Shorten the time needed to obtain a result","Identify the virus before antibodies have developed","Minimise false-positive diagnoses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states confirmatory supplemental testing is highly specific, minimising false-positive diagnoses. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What does the hypothesis verification stage accomplish?","choices":["It gathers data to monitor the patient's response to treatment","It selects which screening test to order first","It confirms the fit of the diagnostic hypotheses with the assembled data","It generates the first list of possible diagnoses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Verification is defined as confirming the fit of the diagnostic hypotheses with the data assembled. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 19"}]};
